"""The five roles that turn a sentence into a verified part.

    Brief Interpreter  -> what was asked, and what was left unsaid
    Requirements Engineer -> checkable requirements, honest assumptions, unknowns
    Clarification Prioritiser -> the few questions actually worth asking
    ForgeIR Designer   -> the parametric document
    Failure Analyst    -> a targeted patch when something fails

The prompts are the product here, as much as the code is, so the reasoning behind
them is recorded alongside them rather than in a commit message.

The theme running through all five: **a number nobody supplied is worse than a
missing number, because it looks checked.** A model asked to design a bracket will
cheerfully invent a 500 N load, and the resulting design will pass every gate,
and the gates will be meaningless. So the roles are built to make the absence of
information a first-class output -- an unknown, an assumption with a risk class,
a blocked requirement -- and the deterministic layers refuse to release on any of
them.
"""

